00:04:33.285 --> 00:04:38.875
So, we're used to volatility,
and we're used to,

00:04:38.875 --> 00:04:42.812
in fact that's in our DNA to take a long
term view through volatile periods.

00:04:42.812 --> 00:04:47.735
So when we look at, I mean last year
the two largest exhaustion or shocks for

00:04:47.735 --> 00:04:50.872
us were the deterioration
in US-China trade.

00:04:50.872 --> 00:04:52.940
We are a global trade bank.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.250
We have a very big business in China,

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.380
we are the biggest international bank
carrying U.S dollars, second biggest.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.620
So we got a big US business,
that's a problem for us.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:08.020
And we contemplate a world where both
China and the U.S byforcade fundamentally,

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.090
we are all thinking about that,
you are thinking about it here,

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.910
and, yeah, we'd be a very different
bank in that environment.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.190
And what do we need to do to prepare for
that?

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:19.280
Because at the end of the day,
we want to remain a very,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.600
very important bank in China and a very
important bank in the United States.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.530
The protests in Hong Kong,
Hong Kong is our single biggest market.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.970
Our name is on the banknotes in Hong Kong.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:32.210
And Hong Kong shut down for six months or
certainly shut down completely for

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.370
three months and
was stressed for three months.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.050
Hong Kong is going to be okay.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.590
And Hong Kong is going to come out the
back end of this, it will be different.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.780
People will say to me,
they'll look at me very earnestly and say,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.830
Hong Kong will never be the same.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.560
Hong Kong never was the same.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.660
Hong Kong is one of the most
dynamic places on the planet,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.370
it changes all the time, and it was
going to change again, in some ways for

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:55.560
the worse, in some ways for the better,
it's a very resilient place.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.170
Let's focus on those things that will
represent the future of Hong Kong.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.540
Let's invest in those and
let's make sure we're there for that.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.210
&gt;&gt; So speaking of volatility,
and even Hong Kong,

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:05.800
you set up some digital activities
in a digital bank based in Asia.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.730
Tell us a little bit about
those activities and

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.020
where you see that headed over
the next five to ten years.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.010
&gt;&gt; One of the things I find very
exciting about Standard Chartered,

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.500
which I think would be off putting for
a lot of people.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:16.710
We are not a big bank.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:20.030
We're a mid sized bank, but we're global.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:23.575
And when I talk to shareholders or
others about Standard Chartered,

00:06:23.575 --> 00:06:27.378
they'll frequently say, do you think
you can compete given your scale?

00:06:27.378 --> 00:06:31.360
I mean, how can you compete with
HSBC who's much bigger than you?

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.780
JP Morgan, where I worked for
25 years, it was much bigger than you.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:35.510
How can you compete?

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:39.872
The answer is, in short, well, we can't
compete if we're competing on scale.

00:06:39.872 --> 00:06:41.600
So we've got to compete on something else.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.700
And we compete on being very local,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.430
whereas not everybody
is as local as we are.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.860
We compete on having
element of global scale.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:52.750
So we have a global network which
is unique to Standard Chartered,

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.590
all across Africa, all across South Asia,
every Asian country, et cetera.

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:59.230
But we also need to be
prepared to be more nimble

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.410
than these people who
are much bigger than we are.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.855
It isn't necessarily in our DNA.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:05.582
I mean,
this is a trait that we've got to acquire.

00:07:05.582 --> 00:07:08.990
But we are in a position in many
of our markets to be a disrupter.

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:09.760
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:13.760
we can be the disrupter who actually has a
brand, who has a physical infrastructure,

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.740
who has licenses, who has good
relationships with regulators.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.730
We're also a highly desirable partner.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:22.481
So in Hong Kong, we partnered
with Hong Kong Telecom and Secha.

00:07:22.481 --> 00:07:27.178
Hong Kong telecom is the ubiquitous
Telco provider, fixed and mobile,

00:07:27.178 --> 00:07:28.800
as well as media.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.840
And Secha Pacific is a travel
agent of the world entirely online

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:36.130
obviously based in China,
but very big in Hong Kong.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:40.720
So with these partners,
we can offer an extremely disruptive model

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.115
in our home market right now, of course,
why would we disrupt ourselves?

00:07:44.115 --> 00:07:48.980
Because it's going to be disrupted one way
or the other, and we want to make sure

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:53.790
that we are positioned to be a not just a
survivor, but a thriver in the new market.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.250
And we're also focusing on
a segment of the market which is

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.370
probably less penetrated,
which is the mass market.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:00.920
So we're building a digital
bank in Hong Kong,

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.030
I would hope that we're in a position
to do something similar in Singapore,

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.400
which is another one of our home markets.

00:08:07.665 --> 00:08:12.150
We got very interesting
partnerships in China with

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:17.140
some of the tech behemoths who really
want to capture part of the financial

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:21.000
services profitability without
being a regulated bank, so

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.520
there's opportunities for us to
partner in digital initiatives there.

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:28.780
We have a venture lab that
is in the process of,

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:32.450
we just partnered up with
a group out of Australia that is

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:35.700
getting into the merchant acquiring
business across Southeast Asia.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.760
Yeah, this is a start up, it's a distinct
venture within our organization.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:45.020
So it doesn't have to deal with any
of the Standard Chartered legacy.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:49.145
But it gets to leverage all
the Standard Chartered competence.

00:08:49.145 --> 00:08:54.546
It get funny that I hank out my,
talking with the Hong Kong Virtual Bank

00:08:54.546 --> 00:08:59.033
team who said,
we're separate from the rest of the bank,

00:08:59.033 --> 00:09:05.190
we're in a fancy, we work style location,
we operate in the valley way.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:08.654
But, sometimes the bank can insinuate
itself into our processes and

00:09:08.654 --> 00:09:10.920
policies make us though, I can see that.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.754
Let's see how we can work through that.

00:09:14.754 --> 00:09:16.412
And who is the competition, right?

00:09:16.412 --> 00:09:19.337
When we're competing with Alibaba,
we're competing with Sanghan.

00:09:19.337 --> 00:09:21.053
They don't have the problem,

00:09:21.053 --> 00:09:25.360
their mothership it doesn't have
this regulated bureaucracy.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.800
Okay, so I guess they're going to
launch way before we will,

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.150
because they can sort of move so
much faster.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:31.360
They said no no, we're probably
going to launch ahead of them.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:32.670
So, why is that?

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:36.962
Well, what they don't have is the systems
that can do the customer's due diligence,

00:09:36.962 --> 00:09:37.780
and know your client.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:39.580
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Sort of checking up front.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:43.585
And we can just do a drag and drop out
of the main bank into the Virtual Bank.

00:09:43.585 --> 00:09:45.158
And say, I get it.

00:09:45.158 --> 00:09:50.900
So we are able to save six months of
development time because we actually

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:55.380
invest in a couple billion dollars into
the world class compliance system.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.290
Whether we love the fact that
we have to do that or not,

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:58.710
it doesn't matter, we have it.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:09:59.730
And the other guys don't have that,

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.200
they're going to have to
build that out themselves.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.331
&gt;&gt; It's an interesting
dynamic you just described.

00:10:02.331 --> 00:10:03.935
You look at the history of the bank,

00:10:03.935 --> 00:10:06.658
follow started by two
people from Scotland.

00:10:06.658 --> 00:10:08.950
Largely doing business in Africa and
throughout Asia, and

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.020
you talked about being
in the local market.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.020
How do you think about that?

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.040
How should the world view
Standard Chartered Bank?

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.510
Where is your local market, and
where are your home markets?

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:21.580
You're based in London, how do you
communicate that to the rest of the world?

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:25.300
&gt;&gt; It's a challenge, and when I think
about the structural challenges and

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:29.890
disadvantages that we have, we don't
the single big profitable home market.

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.610
We are very big in Hong Kong,
it's about a third of our profitability.

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:37.700
But if you look at other big global banks,
HSBC is 80% or

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:42.830
something greater in China,
JP Morgan IS 80% US.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:48.548
And DBS, which is the big bank
in Singapore is 80% Singapore.

00:10:48.548 --> 00:10:52.501
So, on the one hand, there's our challenge
and we don't have that home market that

00:10:52.501 --> 00:10:56.430
they can sort of feed the profits
into other markets as you're growing.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:00.651
And so we've got to find ways to be
creative and innovative in other ways to

00:11:00.651 --> 00:11:04.890
leverage the fact that actually what
we have is a network that's unique.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:12.510
So, I mean the two Scottish guys they
planted a lot of flags back in 1858.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:15.087
&gt;&gt; So one final question for you,
if we look forward ten years from now,

00:11:15.087 --> 00:11:17.975
if you and I are sitting here in 2030,
what do you think the bank looks like

00:11:17.975 --> 00:11:20.484
then, what are the biggest differences and
what will be the same?

00:11:20.484 --> 00:11:23.679
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we do this kind of
exercise from time to time and

00:11:23.679 --> 00:11:26.395
we said what was finance
going to look like?

00:11:26.395 --> 00:11:31.275
And we've got this simplistic view that
the transactional elements of finance will

00:11:31.275 --> 00:11:34.630
be what we drive into community platforms.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:38.960
And we are either going to own or
partially own some of those platforms,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.170
which some of them have
already taken over the world.

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.654
So it's unlikely that we're
going to be owning a ubiquitous

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.947
platform in China anytime soon, or

00:11:47.947 --> 00:11:51.240
we're going to be providing services
in terms of the mass market.

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.424
And in terms of that
transaction element to banking,

00:11:53.424 --> 00:11:55.371
we're going to be providing
services into that.

00:11:55.371 --> 00:11:57.830
And those services
are going to be databased.

00:11:57.830 --> 00:11:59.585
So our massive investment has been in

00:12:00.665 --> 00:12:04.275
enabling digital services that
the data enlightened, and

00:12:04.275 --> 00:12:07.065
we've got a lot of examples where
this is working really well for us.

00:12:07.065 --> 00:12:09.035
But it's a very different kind of model.

00:12:09.035 --> 00:12:11.890
Thankfully in that segment of the market
today, we don't make much money.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.670
So we don't have a lot
of stake at stake there.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:16.920
There's another part of
the financial life though,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:20.030
that will have a heavy
element of trust and advice.

00:12:20.030 --> 00:12:22.890
And I think when individuals
are thinking about their health,

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:25.660
their wealth, their retirement and
the education of their children,

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:29.750
maybe the purchase of their home,
major life decisions.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:34.150
I think for a long time after the computer
is smarter than than the human being,

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:35.870
will still want to have
some human interaction.

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:37.660
And we're making that bet.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:38.580
We've got that brand.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:43.290
So we're leveraging that brand locally and
investing in that trust asset heavily, and

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.580
all the digital tools that go around that.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:48.710
And I think that applies to a big
chunk of the corporate business.

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:52.030
We're roughly half retail
half corporate roughly.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:56.670
And our corporate clients rely on us
to execute a whole range of things, but

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:57.910
mostly cross border things.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.035
And we're a cross border network bank.

00:13:00.035 --> 00:13:02.023
And we've got this this network, and

00:13:02.023 --> 00:13:06.435
we can imagine a world where regulation in
Africa is the same as regulation in China,

00:13:06.435 --> 00:13:09.686
is the same as regulation in Korea,
but we're not there now.

00:13:09.686 --> 00:13:11.986
And we're not going to be there,
I think in my lifetime or

00:13:11.986 --> 00:13:13.140
maybe even your lifetime.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:19.840
So, there's complexity that comes from
helping companies work on a global stage.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.140
Those complexities are getting greater,
not lesser,

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:26.340
with anti globalization movements and
trade barriers and nationalism.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.800
And we're excellent at
navigating those things.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.010
So this is good getting back into the,
not so much the transactional part of

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:35.260
the financial services, but something
where you have some unique insight.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:38.270
So 30 years from now we'll
be delivering that package.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:41.000
&gt;&gt; The lack of a home base
might actually be an asset.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.930
And an unfair competitive advantage for
you going forward because you'll

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.100
understand the broader
geographical issues.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.446
&gt;&gt; Maybe.
&gt;&gt; All right, Bill Winter, CEO,

00:13:47.446 --> 00:13:49.619
Standard Chartered, thank you so
much for coming to Stanford,

00:13:49.619 --> 00:13:50.596
a pleasure to have you here.

00:13:50.596 --> 00:13:51.894
&gt;&gt; Thank you for having me.

00:13:51.894 --> 00:13:56.521
[MUSIC]

